<title>ITALY: ITALIAN SOY PLANTINGS UP BY 57.2 PCT - MINISTRY.</title>
<headline>ITALIAN SOY PLANTINGS UP BY 57.2 PCT - MINISTRY.</headline>
<dateline>ROME 1997-08-06</dateline>
<text>
<p>The amount of land given over to growing soybeans in Italy has increased by 57.2 percent this year against 1996 levels while the soft wheat area has fallen by 16.5 percent, the agriculture ministry said in a statement.</p>
<p>Using latest satellite images, the ministry gave the following breakdown of sown land (figures in hectares).</p>
<p>    CROP		1997		 1996	   PCT DIFFERENCE</p>
<p> Durum wheat     1,644,324	  1,711,171	   -3.9%</p>
<p> Soft wheat	  566,798	    678,843	  -16.5%</p>
<p> Barley		353,551	    359,161	   -1.6%</p>
<p> Rapeseed	     39,250	     55,014	  -28.7%</p>
<p> Maize	     1,219,449	  1,133,483	   +7.6%</p>
<p> Sugarbeet	   332,765	    265,658	  +25.3%</p>
<p> Sunflower	   216,782	    167,366	  +29.5%</p>
<p> Soybean	     292,590	    186,091	  +57.2%</p>
<p>Notes:</p>
<p>- The ministry said the maize, sunflower, soybean and sugarbeet data was provisional, with full figures not due until next month.</p>
<p>- It said the increase in the oilseed area, especially soybean and sunflower, had been expected and could be partly explained by more favourable subsidies currently on offer from the European Union.</p>
</text>
